Good, now I have a clear picture of all the data sources. Let me design the H14 V-shape trajectory check. The hypothesis:

H14 Hypothesis: BALLISTIC edges (h7v2-kept) that have a "V-shape" trajectory through a hand region — i.e., the source tracklet's tail + the target tracklet's head + linear interpolation in the gap form a V that dips into a hand region — may actually be HIDDEN catch-throws that the strict h7v2 rule missed (because the ball was further than 108px at endpoint, or the slope was gentler, etc.).

The H7v2 reclassification rule requires:
- end_dist <= 108 AND end_slope < -1.0 (catch signature)
- OR start_dist <= 108 AND start_slope > 1.0 (throw signature)

A V-shape check would be more permissive: even if the endpoint distance is 110-130 px (just outside the 108 threshold), if the full trajectory shows a clear V toward the hand, it's still a hidden catch-throw.

