So:
- t27 ends at (617, 392) at f=673 — moving down at ~3 px/frame
- t28 starts at (604, 306) at f=678 — already at the apex of a rising arc, moving up at ~9 px/frame
- 5 frames of "gap" but the ball teleports 87 px in 5 frames — that's NOT a smooth catch+throw.

The t28 trajectory starts at (604, 306) with vy ~-9 (rising). A real catch+throw would have t28 starting near the hand with v_y close to 0 or slightly negative. Instead t28 starts already in flight (apex was before f=678, and the ball is rising fast).

This is the key insight: a real catch+throw has the new tracklet starting with low velocity (the ball is just leaving the hand). A tracklet break (mid-air continuation) has the new tracklet starting with ballistic velocity (the ball is already in flight).

