27→28 YouTube (FP per H14):
- t27 source tail: at (618, 403) → (617, 392) — slowly rising far from both hands (L=88-95, R=179-189)
- t28 target head: starts at (604, 306) → (595, 260) — rising fast in upper region, far from right hand (R=269-326)
- The ball is high up in the air in BOTH segments — no hand interaction visible
- The source has 4 frames of consistent slow rise (the 0.5 px/frame up), target has 6 frames of consistent fast rise (~9 px/frame up). These are two different arcs, not a catch+throw.

The key discriminator: hand-distance at source tail AND target head. Real catch-throws have BOTH endpoints near a hand (within reach ~108 px). Tracklet breaks have one or both endpoints far from a hand (ball is in mid-air).

