OK so the vision tool says it's a false positive. Now the interesting thing: my H20 in-hand check (≤30 px) failed to detect it because the actual tracklet endpoint distances are 50-80 px. The ball is at the hand visually but the detection is jittering 50-80 px from the wrist.

This is a different failure mode than "both endpoints in the same hand" — it's "endpoint positions are noisy/jittery when the ball is held" and the strict V-shape picks up crossing patterns as V-shapes.

Let me look at the visual ground truth more carefully. The frames in the contact sheet are 36, 37, 38, 40 (t4 source) and 43, 44 (t8 target). The V-apex is at f=40 (min_d=2.97, hand=right). But t4's last 3 frames (f=33, 34, 35) are 58-85 px from the right hand, and t8's first frames (f=43, 44, 45) are 73-75 px from the left hand. So the V-apex interpolated to ~3 px from the right hand is an artifact of linear interpolation between the last src frame and first tgt frame — both of which are >58 px from any hand.

This is a V-shape false positive caused by linear interpolation, not by the data itself. A more robust check would be: the V-apex must be at a position that is reachable from both endpoints in physically plausible motion. The current "min_d < 50" + ratio > 1.5" check doesn't catch this because the linear-interpolated V-apex is a synthetic point.

But wait — the V-apex check IS the test, and it passed. The "false positive" is a known limitation of the V-shape classifier. My H20 in-hand check is a different, complementary check.

The fundamental issue is that H17's strict V-shape has multiple failure modes:
1. Both endpoints in hand (24→27 youtube — caught by H20)
2. Linear interpolation V-apex artifact (4→8, 66→68 — NOT caught by H20)
3. Source only in hand, target airborne (1→10, 10→11 — only one-sided in-hand)
4. Apex at shoulder/torso, not hand (24→27 — different geometry)

H20 catches only #1 cleanly. This is still useful — it's a precision-preserving filter that doesn't break anything. Let me also try a different filter: vel_jump_check to add another precision dimension.

