Hmm — wait. Looking at the V_SHALLOW results (2/2 = 100% FALSE) is surprising because H20/H24 found V_SHALLOW to be more reliable. Let me re-examine. The 39→46 and 6→14 are V_SHALLOW but both got FALSE because the source and target don't form a V.

But actually, looking at the data more carefully:
- 39→46 (V_SHALLOW, min_d=51.9): vision says FALSE, source ball 144 px above hand, target ball 90px right of hand. The min_d=51.9 is from the V-apex to the hand, not source-target distance. The target ball is not near the hand.
- 6→14 (V_SHALLOW, min_d=54.8): source ball moving away from R hand downward; target ball already past the R hand moving up.

So the V-apex is computed as somewhere between source and target, but doesn't necessarily mean both endpoints are at the hand. This is a known limitation of the V-shape recovery approach. The min_d metric measures apex-to-hand distance, not source-target connectivity.

Now, this is a useful negative finding for the H17 V-shape approach: V_SHALLOW doesn't mean the ball is actually close to the hand at both endpoints. Let me write the report. But first, let me also save the verdicts to a permanent file.
