So the apex is actually in the TARGET, not in the gap. That means the source is ABOVE the apex (which is correct: catch descends) and the target is BELOW the apex (which is wrong: the target's first frame is at y=491, apex at y=527, so target IS above apex).

This is a confusing case. The V-apex isn't necessarily the "lowest" point. It's the point closest to the hand. For 13→15, the right hand is at (517, 517). The target (526, 491) at f=138 is 26.6 px from wrist. The target (527, 527) at f=140 is 10 px from wrist. So the V-apex is the point on the trajectory closest to the wrist, which happens to be in the target tracklet at f=140.

This is actually a post-catch point in the target tracklet. The throw is from f=140 onward (target y goes from 527 to 572). So the "V-apex" is actually inside the throw, not the catch.

This means my H30 check is built on a flawed assumption about the V-apex. Let me think about this differently.

The H30 design needs to be: the trajectory must dip toward the hand. Let me use a different approach: the V-apex is the point on the trajectory with min distance to the hand. The source's last frame should be on the OPPOSITE side of the V-apex from the target's first frame. If source and target are both on the same side, the trajectory is monotonic (not V-shaped).

