My earlier analysis was wrong because the script didn't filter by stem. The H33 script correctly filters. So on identical, all 18 multi-tracklet chains have NO tracklet overlap. This means the temporal-overlap signal doesn't work for this chain set.

But the visual QA showed chains 22, 0, 30, 3, 1 are MULTI_BALL_MERGE — these are multi-ball merges without temporal overlap. The balls are visible at the same time but the tracklets are sequenced end-to-start.

Let me reconsider. The tracklet overlap is too strict. A real multi-ball merge doesn't require temporal overlap of consecutive tracklets. What I need is a different signal: at any single frame, how many tracklets from the chain are simultaneously "alive"?

Let me look at chain 22: 7 tids spanning f=507-716. If the tracklets don't overlap temporally, then only 1 ball is "active" at any given frame. But the visual QA shows 3 balls simultaneously visible. How can that be if the tracklets don't overlap?

The answer: the tracklet_features.csv only has 1 row per tid (first/last/slope/etc). It doesn't have per-point data. So the tracklet's "duration" is from first_frame to last_frame, but during that time it might only have a few actual detection points. The actual visible balls may be different from the tracklet IDs.

In other words: a chain with 7 tids all sequenced end-to-start doesn't mean 7 sequential ball appearances — it means 7 fragments that were linked together by hand-region support. The visible balls at any given moment could be 2-3 different ones that the chain doesn't track.

So tracklet overlap is NOT a useful signal. The chain's structure doesn't tell us how many physical balls were visible at any moment.

