Now I see the structure. The "time in air" between CATCH and THROW of the same tracklet is the hold time. The "time in air" between THROW and the next CATCH of the same chain is the flight time.

For a 3-ball cascade, the same ball is held briefly, then thrown, flies for ~3× the beat time, and is caught again. We can compute per-tracklet "air_time" using next CATCH of same chain.

Let me design H45 carefully. There are some complications:
1. The chain is a sequence of catches/throws; a single tracklet has a CATCH at start and THROW at end. The "flight time" of a ball after this THROW is until the next CATCH in the same chain (which may be a different tracklet).
2. We need to assign "ball identity" across catches (the chain order already implies this).

For each chain, I'll compute:
- hold_time[f] = throw_frame - catch_frame (same tracklet)
- flight_time[f] = next_catch_frame - throw_frame (cross-tracklet, same chain)

Then the siteswap digit is flight_time / beat_time. If all catches in a chain have similar hold_time, all throws in a chain have similar flight_time, and the median flight_time/hold_time ratio is roughly 3-5, we have a uniform siteswap pattern.

But the data is too sparse (24 catches on identical, 25 on YouTube). Siteswap is typically applied to a continuous juggling pattern. With this sparse event log, we can only validate whether each catch-throw pair has a "plausible digit" based on the data.

