Very interesting! Notice that the H53b script is using from_tid=to_tid for many pairs (e.g., "8,8", "6,6", "25,25"). That's because the H50 timeline has CATCH and THROW events with the same tid — the H12 v8 event log records the same tracklet's catch and throw as separate events, not different tracklets. The "from_tid" should be the tracklet that catches the ball, and the "to_tid" should be the tracklet that the ball is thrown INTO. But in many H12 v8 events, both endpoints are the same tracklet (because the tracklet spans the entire catch-throw cycle).

This is a fundamental difference between the H50 timeline (which uses CATCH+THROW per-tracklet) and the H52 physics check (which checks source-tail + gap + target-head). For H12 v8 events where the CATCH and THROW are in the same tracklet, the "source" and "target" are literally the same trajectory, so the velocity discontinuity is near 0.

The H50-only drops (chain 13, 23, 36) all have from_tid=to_tid and v_disc < 5, meaning H52 sees "OK" (same tracklet, consistent velocity) but H50 sees "drop" (gap_frames < 10). The H52-only drops include many high-gap pairs that the parabolic fit correctly flags as VIOLATING but H50 keeps (because gap > 10). 

This shows the two filters are largely complementary. H50 catches short-gap events, H52+MIN=2 catches long-gap events with discontinuous velocities. Together they could form a stronger filter.

